WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.000
Elementary-aged astronauts,&nbsp;
fights with wild tigers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.440
and stop-motion horror decades in the making.&nbsp;
From slasher flicks to period piece thrillers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.720
these are the sleeper hit movies of 2022.
 
As the horrors of school shootings have become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.920
terrifying and tragic part of everyday reality for&nbsp;
American youths, it can be difficult to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.600
how to begin to tackle this topic in the context&nbsp;
of a movie. In contrast to the tone-deaf stab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.640
at relevance showrunners made in the "Shooting&nbsp;
Star" episode of Glee, The Fallout tackles the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.680
psychology of a teenager living in the wake of&nbsp;
a school shooting with depth and specificity.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.360
That teenager in question, Vada Cavell,&nbsp;
experiences trauma extending far beyond just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.160
the school shooting event itself. Just trying to&nbsp;
go back to high school becomes an enormous trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.880
and her outlets for controlling her anxiety&nbsp;
aren't always as healthy as they could be.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.280
The remarkable thing about writer-director&nbsp;
Megan Park's work on The Fallout is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.600
sheer variety she lends to each of the&nbsp;
high schoolers depicted in this story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.720
There's never one way to cope with trauma, and the&nbsp;
same can be said for surviving a school shooting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.400
Vada and her friends register as real human beings&nbsp;
thanks to The Fallout taking the time to outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.840
the specific and varied ways they're each moving&nbsp;
forward in their lives after unspeakable horrors.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:24.720
"There isn't a f---ing reason.&nbsp;
None of us should have died."

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.360
Meanwhile, the lead performance&nbsp;
from Jenna Ortega is remarkable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.960
especially her subtle detailed depiction of Vada&nbsp;
shutting out those around her. The Fallout is far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.840
from an easy watch, but it's an unflinching&nbsp;
and emotionally astute piece of filmmaking.
 &nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.920
The people we lionize in society are just&nbsp;
people like everyone else. It can be easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.280
to forget that in everyday life, but Asghar&nbsp;
Farhadi's A Hero, which played at Cannes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.800
in 2021 but hit Prime Video in 2022,&nbsp;
is a perfect reminder of this truth.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.320
The film tells the story of Rahim, played by Amir&nbsp;
Jadidi, who was recently released from prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.560
As he begins to rebuild his life, he returns&nbsp;
a bag of money to its rightful owner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.760
making him a hero in the eyes of his neighbors&nbsp;
and community. However, Rahim's past comes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.240
to haunt him as dissenting voices begin&nbsp;
to question the validity of his actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.960
Not only does this place his new reputation&nbsp;
in danger, but it also begins to eat away at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.600
Rahim's ability to secure new employment&nbsp;
and stake out a post-prison existence.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.120
The same nuance and richly detailed&nbsp;
characterizations that Farhadi brought to his 2011&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.440
masterpiece A Separation are once again present in&nbsp;
A Hero. These intricacies make the ensuing tragedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.880
of the plotline all the more compelling to watch,&nbsp;
as does the delicately thoughtful camerawork from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.400
Farhadi and cinematographer Ali Ghazi. The&nbsp;
cinematography is especially unforgettable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.040
in the film's closing shot, which leaves&nbsp;
viewers with a sense of unshakeable despair.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.560
Boasting a 96% score on Rotten Tomatoes, and&nbsp;
bizarrely overlooked for a Best International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.480
Feature Oscar nomination, A Hero is another&nbsp;
Asghar Farhadi gem that is well worth watching.
 &nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.160
From A.I. Artificial Intelligence to&nbsp;
Bicentennial Man, there have been plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.440
of movies navigating the relationship between&nbsp;
human families and robotic companions. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.320
none of them have been quite like After&nbsp;
Yang, the latest film from director Kogonada.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.600
This film chronicles a family&nbsp;
led by their father, Jake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.400
played by Colin Farrell, figuring out&nbsp;
what to do once their longtime robot Yang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.880
abruptly shuts down. While determining the&nbsp;
next steps for this mechanical being that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.000
was so important to his family, Jake begins to&nbsp;
uncover extraordinary things in Yang's memories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.560
This discovery suggests he was even more&nbsp;
complex and human than they could've imagined.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.080
The contemplative style of filmmaking&nbsp;
Kogonada applied to his 2017 film Columbus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.200
is alive and well in its follow up, but After&nbsp;
Yang is far from a reprise of the director's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.160
previous cinematic effort. The ingenious&nbsp;
use of shifting aspect ratios, paired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.280
with Kogonada's striking editing techniques,&nbsp;
gives After Yang a distinct visual identity.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.400
Meanwhile, the quiet pieces of unique technology&nbsp;
and architecture surrounding the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.960
suggest a broader near-future world without&nbsp;
overwhelming the story. The filmmaking and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.000
melancholy ambiance are so captivating that&nbsp;
they make this movie feel like the very first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.280
film to ever tackle the idea of distinctly&nbsp;
human traits lurking inside a robotic being.
 &nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.000
The tale of Cyrano de Bergerac&nbsp;
has been captivating audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.040
in various mediums for well&nbsp;
over a century. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.000
no one has told this adventurous story in&nbsp;
quite the way Joe Wright does in Cyrano.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.000
"Pleasure to meet you. Cyrano de Bergerac."

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.080
This 2022 film brings the story&nbsp;
to life as a musical headlined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.800
by Peter Dinklage in the titular role.
The story of an expert swordsman who believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.240
himself to be too hideous to be loved by the woman&nbsp;
of his dreams, it's a tragic romantic yarn that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.840
bursts off the screen with palpable pathos. This&nbsp;
is a movie that wears its sentimental belief in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.040
old-fashioned love on its sleeve, and it's all&nbsp;
the better for such a cynicism-free execution.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.080
This straightforward approach to the romance is&nbsp;
mirrored by the confident execution of the music.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.800
From random conversations punctuated by&nbsp;
melody, to letters read aloud through lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.280
actor Haley Bennett's sensual crooning,&nbsp;
the songs spring from the lips of cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.160
members at any given opportunity. Cyrano&nbsp;
has no qualms about beating its own drum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.040
which comes in especially handy during its&nbsp;
emotionally devastating musical sequences.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.440
Some may find it more hokey than moving,&nbsp;
but if you like old-fashioned romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.400
dramas that throw subtlety and caution to&nbsp;
the wind, then Cyrano will deeply move you.
 &nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.400
In the grand tradition of Rope or Buried, The&nbsp;
Outfit largely takes place in one location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.600
In this case, that singular backdrop is the&nbsp;
clothing store of cutter Leonard Burling, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.360
by Mark Rylance. He prides himself on making the&nbsp;
finest clothes possible for his customers, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.920
as how he stays out of the way of the mobsters&nbsp;
who use his place to hold money and letters.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.040
One late night, this shop becomes a hotbed of&nbsp;
activity when gangster Richie Boyle is killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.440
by a fellow mobster. Thus begins a night&nbsp;
of increasingly dangerous activity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.120
sees this mild-mannered expert in fashion using&nbsp;
all his wits to stay alive. Writer and director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.000
Graham Moore does fine work utilizing this&nbsp;
simple premise and minimalist environments to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.080
create an engaging crime thriller.
 
Part of the film's charm comes from Moore's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.360
willingness to have dark fun with his material.&nbsp;
The R-rating provided the filmmaker freedom to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:35.040
include realistic bursts of bloody mayhem and&nbsp;
impactful dialogue laden with coarse language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.760
Mark Rylance's years of stage experience&nbsp;
serves him well within the confined set, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:45.440
he manages to keep viewers engaged with minimal&nbsp;
resources. Unsurprisingly, the Oscar winner proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.120
the perfect lead for the tense thriller. 
 
Some movies only offer excess spectacle but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.880
no heart, others merely schmaltz but no scope.&nbsp;
RRR [R-R-R], an epic blockbuster hailing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:02.560
director S. S. Rajamouli, wisely decides to&nbsp;
deliver both of these ingredients at once.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:09.600
This story concerns two men, Komaram Bheem&nbsp;
and Alluri Sitarama Raju, in 1920s Delhi. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.720
duo sparks up a deeply passionate friendship&nbsp;
without realizing the other's true identity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.280
Bheem is on a mission to rescue a young girl&nbsp;
who was kidnapped from his village while Raju&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:25.440
is a cop who demonstrates deep loyalty to brutally&nbsp;
cruel colonial figures like Governor Scott Buxton.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.760
RRR delivers one riotously over-the-top action&nbsp;
scene after another. Any pretense towards being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.480
grounded has been tossed to the wind in&nbsp;
favor of delightfully maximalist chaos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.360
At the same time, there's a genuineness to&nbsp;
how Rajamouli depicts the friendship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.440
Bheem and Raju that makes it impossible&nbsp;
not to get wrapped up in their bond.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.880
Depictions of this duo standing strong and&nbsp;
fighting back against cruel colonial forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.720
also stir the soul. There's a beating heart to&nbsp;
RRR that lends deep humanity to a movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.840
also features exploding trains and flaming&nbsp;
carriages. Tossing in a bevy of remarkably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.520
catchy tunes like "Naatu Naatu," and "RRR"&nbsp;
becomes a cinematic delight one can't miss.
 &nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:14.720
Writer and director Richard Linklater returns to&nbsp;
the world of rotoscope filmmaking with Apollo 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.560
1/2: A Space Age Childhood. For this production,&nbsp;
Linklater splits the story between the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.360
and the fantastical. Part of the runtime is&nbsp;
concerned with chronicling the exploits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.800
young Stanley growing up in 1960s Houston,&nbsp;
Texas, when the Space Race was everywhere.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.760
The film is somewhat autobiographical for&nbsp;
Linklater, and authenticity reverberates off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.480
these recollections of the highs and lows of&nbsp;
such an important historical period. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:46.800
Apollo 10 1/2 also ventures into a heightened&nbsp;
yarn about Stanley being called in by NASA for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:52.080
a top-secret mission where he gets sent to the&nbsp;
moon before Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:56.960
The combination of the disparate narratives&nbsp;
in Apollo 10 1/2 is fairly seamless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.800
and it's greatly assisted by Jack&nbsp;
Black's wall-to-wall narration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.960
which bridges the gap between Stanley's home
life and his voyage to the stars.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.160
"Okay pause. Let's forget about all this for&nbsp;
now. We'll come back to this part later."

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.560
Even better, Linklater injects amusingly specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.360
details into Stanley's reminiscing about&nbsp;
childhood elements like his uber-paranoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.600
grandmother or how his mom could make&nbsp;
one ham dinner last an entire week.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.640
The entire story is told through nicely&nbsp;
realized hand-drawn animated rotoscoping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.720
a welcome return of an artform Linklater used so&nbsp;
well on earlier projects like A Scanner Darkly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.240
With these qualities, it becomes quite easy to&nbsp;
recommend Apollo 10 1/2 as a trip worth taking.
 &nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:47.040
When director Panah Panahi's Hit&nbsp;
the Road begins, it appears that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:51.840
the film will focus on a married couple and&nbsp;
their two sons on a road trip across Iran.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:58.320
Slowly but surely, though, Panahi peels back&nbsp;
the layers on this family and their excursion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.440
reveal deeper, darker truths. This is anything&nbsp;
but a normal road trip, and it quickly becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.680
apparent that we're watching an exercise in&nbsp;
quiet tragedy masquerading as familial normalcy.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.160
The film's power is especially apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.640
in how each of the lead characters gets&nbsp;
a unique and distinctive point of view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.600
The intentionally cramped confines of the&nbsp;
narrative allow us time to get to know everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:27.440
in this family while the restrained camerawork&nbsp;
offers subtle glimpses into their interior worlds.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.320
Panahi's strengths as a filmmaker are&nbsp;
further reinforced by how well he can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.320
incorporate bursts of dark humor into Hit the&nbsp;
Road without undercutting its ominous tone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:44.320
Above all else, Pantea Panahiha's performance&nbsp;
as the unnamed mother of this family may be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.840
ultimate reason to see the film, especially her&nbsp;
work in the film's devastating final sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:55.040
Performances like that, just like movies as&nbsp;
good as Hit the Road, are far from commonplace.
 &nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.240
A24's horror fare has often&nbsp;
gone in artsy directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:06.560
with an emphasis on long stretches of silence,&nbsp;
meditative wide shots, and abstract imagery. X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:12.080
a title from the studio thrust into theaters&nbsp;
in March 2022, goes down a different lane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:18.400
with its horror influences being more in the vein&nbsp;
of The Texas Chainsaw Massacre. With a 1970s porno&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:24.080
shoot as the backdrop, X is a different beast&nbsp;
compared to Hereditary or The Witch, but no less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.680
well crafted. Like many of its predecessors,&nbsp;
this A24 horror film is a compelling watch.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.000
As for the plot, the film follows a group&nbsp;
of young filmmakers as they show up at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.440
Texas ranch to make an adult movie. Needless to&nbsp;
say, things do not go according to plan. Writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.840
and director Ti West isn't reinventing&nbsp;
the mold here, but he doesn't need to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.880
This is a film that conveys a giddy glee in&nbsp;
playing with the grisly storytelling hallmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:53.920
of the slasher movie genre, and getting&nbsp;
audiences all riled up with anticipation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.920
by overtly teeing up gruesome&nbsp;
ways for characters to perish.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.480
In between all that fun, there's even an&nbsp;
occasionally poignant undercurrent to X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.000
about mortality and who modern society deems&nbsp;
attractive. These themes add some heft to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.080
proceedings, but if you just want to&nbsp;
watch X for the gore and titillation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:16.400
there's plenty of that to satisfy. X is&nbsp;
a departure from the standards of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.600
other scary A24 titles, but following its&nbsp;
own creative path serves it mighty well.
 &nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.880
Jerrod Carmichael not only stars in On&nbsp;
the Count of Three, he also makes his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:32.560
directorial debut with this extremely heavy&nbsp;
film centered around suicide. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:37.760
the film is about lifelong best friends Val,&nbsp;
played by Carmichael, and Kevin, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:42.400
Christopher Abbott, committing to a pact that&nbsp;
they'll kill themselves by the end of the day.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:46.560
It's a brutal and heavy premise told&nbsp;
with deftly executed dark humor.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.000
"I don't listen to Alanis Morisette&nbsp;
when I'm going through a breakup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.200
and I'm not listening to Papa Roach&nbsp;
on the day I'm gonna kill myself."

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.480
Much of this comes from how screenwriters&nbsp;
Ari Katcher and Ryan Welch pull no punches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:02.800
in trying to make the audience understand&nbsp;
these characters and their complex motives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.400
We're always in tune with the deep&nbsp;
psychological trauma that's making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:11.520
them believe that suicide is a reasonable&nbsp;
option to escape their tormented existences.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:17.280
The empathetic nature of On the Count of Three is&nbsp;
especially apparent in the mesmerizing performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:23.200
by Christopher Abbott as Kevin. A man just barely&nbsp;
keeping his long-simmering pain under the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:27.360
Abbott can break your heart with a facial&nbsp;
expression, and both he and Carmichael have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:32.240
authentic lived-in chemistry with one another.&nbsp;
On the Count of Three is not an easy watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:37.520
far from it. But those who are able to take it&nbsp;
in will be gifted with an impressive first foray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:42.160
into filmmaking from Carmichael, as well as one&nbsp;
of the year's best performances from Abbott.
 &nbsp;

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:49.040
In her second feature-length directorial effort,&nbsp;
director Audrey Diwan delivers an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.520
piece of cinema in the form of Happening.&nbsp;
This production tells the story of Anne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:59.120
played by Anamaria Vartolomei, a&nbsp;
young woman living in France in 1963.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:03.280
Her hefty ambitions for the future get&nbsp;
upended when she learns she's pregnant.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:08.080
All Anne wants to do is get an abortion and move&nbsp;
on with her future, but abortions are outlawed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.280
in France in this era. Now Anne has to sneak&nbsp;
around, speaking in secrecy and using subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:18.080
glances to reveal her true feelings while&nbsp;
keeping her crisis from everyone around her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:23.280
It's an intense storyline that Diwan brings&nbsp;
to life with subtle but impactful power.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.640
Among the film's many strengths is Vartolomei's&nbsp;
performance in the lead role. Handed a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.920
largely eschewing overt dialogue, Vartolomei&nbsp;
works wonders in conveying Anne's thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:39.680
process via quieter means. Diwan's direction&nbsp;
is also remarkable, with visual details like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.520
a constricted aspect ratio illustrating how&nbsp;
confined Anne feels in her day-to-day life.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:50.080
All of these thoughtful touches add up to&nbsp;
a movie you can't shake off once it's over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:53.680
Happening isn't just a sign of&nbsp;
Diwan's powers as a director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:59.120
it's also an exceptional ode to what human&nbsp;
beings endure in the face of inhumane laws.
 &nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:06.320
In production for decades, Mad God is a labor of&nbsp;
love from Phil Tippett, an icon of the stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:11.440
animation industry. It's a miracle this film&nbsp;
ever managed to see the light of day considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.960
that it took crowdsourcing to get the funds&nbsp;
necessary to bring this warped vision to life.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:22.080
All of that time, effort, and money were&nbsp;
spent on a feature that largely eschews plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:27.360
in favor of delivering increasingly disturbing&nbsp;
visions of a post-apocalyptic world where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.680
suffering is omnipresent. Doctors&nbsp;
deprive people of their guts rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:37.280
than save anyone while husks of human beings&nbsp;
are created, often solely to die in the abyss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:41.920
Mad God isn't just dark, it's relentlessly bleak.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:47.040
While such a tone won't be for everyone, those&nbsp;
that can get on Tippett's wavelength will be happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:52.720
with what Mad God delivers. Running just a hair&nbsp;
under 80 minutes before the credits begin, Mad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:59.040
God manages to constantly deliver unpredictable&nbsp;
and unnerving imagery brimming with imagination.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.520
The various textures provided by the&nbsp;
stop-motion medium make the film appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.680
more viscerally appalling and unique.&nbsp;
It's hard to comprehend an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:12.480
computer-generated nightmare world like&nbsp;
this one being nearly as impactful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:18.800
just as it's difficult to imagine many other 2022&nbsp;
titles being as lastingly chilling as Mad God.
 &nbsp;

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:24.880
If you're going to watch anyone talk in a hotel&nbsp;
room for 90 minutes, it might as well be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:30.560
instantly compelling performer like Emma Thompson.&nbsp;
She takes on the role of Nancy Stokes in Good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.080
Luck to You, Leo Grande, while Daryl McCormack&nbsp;
plays the titular part of a male sex worker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:41.440
After her husband passes away, Nancy decides&nbsp;
to finally engage in the sexual exploits that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:46.000
have been a mystery to her for decades,&nbsp;
hiring Leo for an hour or two of passion.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.000
"Number 3: we do a 69, if that's what it's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:55.440
called? I don't know. Um 4:&nbsp;
Me on top. 5: doggy style."

00:16:55.440 --> 00:17:00.640
What sounds like a simple arrangement becomes&nbsp;
anything but as Stokes begins to open up more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.680
and more to Grande. Katy Brand's screenplay&nbsp;
ensures that the dialogue between these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:10.800
characters is as humorous as it is revealing.&nbsp;
The boldly intimate scope of the project, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:15.760
is largely confined to one hotel room, proves&nbsp;
to be a boon to Good Luck to You, Leo Grande,&nbsp;

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:21.360
offering viewers a chance to understand the finer&nbsp;
nuances of this duo without any distractions.

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:26.400
What's more, the film allows moviegoers the&nbsp;
opportunity to appreciate the exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:31.360
lead performances by Thompson and McCormack.&nbsp;
Good Luck to You, Leo Grande is the newest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:35.120
reminder that you don't need an enormous&nbsp;
story to make an engrossing movie.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:40.560
One of the first scenes in Neptune Frost&nbsp;
depicts people chanting and banging on drums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:45.440
in the grim domain of a mine where a worker&nbsp;
has just perished. These performers are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:49.520
aberration in this environment, but&nbsp;
that's the point. Within this sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:54.560
Neptune Frost has established that music can&nbsp;
appear anywhere. The same is true for things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:59.680
like the spirit of rebellion, empathy towards&nbsp;
other human beings, and one's true identity.

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:02.800
No matter how much oppressive&nbsp;
societies try to stifle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:07.360
or even wipe out, these forces, they&nbsp;
will endure. Hinging on this concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:11.840
Neptune Frost reveals itself to be a&nbsp;
stirring battle cry in cinematic form.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:18.560
Directors Saul Williams and Anisia Uzeyman tell&nbsp;
the tale of Neptune, played by both Elvis Ngabo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:23.840
and Cheryl Isheja, who joins a hacking&nbsp;
initiative. There's a visual audacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:28.240
particularly when it comes to costume and&nbsp;
production design. In fact, this might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:33.680
the only film to feature a major character in&nbsp;
an outfit made completely out of keyboard keys.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:38.000
Such elements reflect how this film&nbsp;
focuses on a collection of human beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:42.160
who can finally be themselves away&nbsp;
from oppressive systemic forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:47.840
Insightful dialogue by the film's protagonists&nbsp;
reinforces the movie's touching exploration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:53.040
individuals finally discovering the space to&nbsp;
embrace their own identity. There's a beating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:59.200
captivating heart to Neptune Frost, which informs&nbsp;
its ceaseless creativity and unwavering rebellion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:04.080
against fascism. Even the most jaded viewers&nbsp;
won't be able to resist something this stirring.
 &nbsp;

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:10.400
The way racism is ingrained into the fabric&nbsp;
of America and its institutions is such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:16.400
overwhelming part of reality that even beginning&nbsp;
to approach it in film can intimidate filmmakers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:22.560
The documentary Who We Are: A Chronicle of Racism&nbsp;
in America, hailing from directors Emily and Sarah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:28.000
Kunstler, provides an expansive look at the&nbsp;
past and present of racism's impact on the U.S.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:33.600
The story is told through the words of ACLU&nbsp;
deputy legal director Jeffery Robinson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.200
The film split between clips of him&nbsp;
giving a talk about the history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:42.080
America's racism to a packed crowd, and&nbsp;
segments depicting Robinson talking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:45.680
various American citizens about&nbsp;
this urgently relevant topic.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:51.280
Who We Are is a heavy watch, as it should&nbsp;
be. The filmmakers and Robinson refuse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:55.760
distill the horrors of bigotry to&nbsp;
just flat readings of statistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:59.360
There's a passionate fervor in&nbsp;
Robinson's public speaking skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:04.880
as he dives into the nuances of how racism has&nbsp;
informed key parts of America's foundation.

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:09.680
"Who controls the past controls&nbsp;
the future, and your children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:13.360
they are being taught that slavery&nbsp;
was a side issue in the Civil War."

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:18.720
Meanwhile, his interviews remind viewers&nbsp;
of the human costs of systemic racism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:23.040
being normalized. The overwhelming&nbsp;
reality of intolerance in America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:32.560
is superbly reflected within&nbsp;
the lens of Who We Are.

